#
# TTA Notifications v0.6 POC demo (docs/tta-notifications-v06/)
#
# Two knooppunten from docker compose:
#   - Plataan (URA 00000010), Subscription Server / sender:
#       internal http://localhost:8081, public http://localhost:8080
#   - Zonnebloem (URA 00000020), Subscription Client / receiver:
#       internal http://localhost:8181, public http://localhost:8180
#
# The "vendor"/XIS side of both organisations is these plain-JSON internal
# calls — note that nothing below except step 6 (deliberately showing the
# wire) requires any knowledge of the FHIR Subscriptions Backport format.
#
# IntelliJ IDEA HTTP Client docs: https://www.jetbrains.com/help/idea/http-client-in-product-code-editor.html
#

### Step 0: the agreed SubscriptionTopic, discoverable at the server (design-time contract)
GET http://localhost:8080/fhir/SubscriptionTopic/nl-task-notified-pull
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Step 1: Plataan's XIS registers the authorization basis when the care
### relationship is created (who may be notified of what). POC: recorded and
### logged by the stub hook; real GF Authorization is out of scope.
POST http://localhost:8081/subscription/authorizations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "receiverUra": "00000020",
  "topic": "http://fhir.nl/SubscriptionTopic/nl-task-notified-pull"
}

### Step 2: Zonnebloem's XIS subscribes at Plataan — in-band creation via one
### internal call (the knooppunt builds the backport Subscription, resolves the
### partner endpoint, POSTs it and handles the handshake).
POST http://localhost:8181/subscription/intents
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "partnerUra": "00000010",
  "topic": "http://fhir.nl/SubscriptionTopic/nl-task-notified-pull",
  "payloadMode": "id-only",
  "heartbeatPeriodSeconds": 60
}

### Step 2b: subscription state on both sides — wait for status "active"
### (handshake done). Run on Plataan:
GET http://localhost:8081/subscription/subscriptions

### ...and on Zonnebloem:
GET http://localhost:8181/subscription/subscriptions

### Step 3 (option A): seed a COW Coordination Task in Plataan's tenant; the
### knooppunt's poller (5s interval) detects it and notifies Zonnebloem.
POST http://localhost:7050/fhir/plataan-patients/Task
Content-Type: application/fhir+json

{
  "resourceType": "Task",
  "status": "requested",
  "intent": "order",
  "code": {
    "coding": [
      { "system": "http://hl7.org/fhir/CodeSystem/task-code", "code": "fulfill" },
      { "system": "http://snomed.info/sct", "code": "308292007", "display": "Transfer of care" }
    ]
  },
  "owner": {
    "identifier": { "system": "http://fhir.nl/fhir/NamingSystem/ura", "value": "00000020" }
  },
  "requester": {
    "identifier": { "system": "http://fhir.nl/fhir/NamingSystem/ura", "value": "00000010" }
  }
}

### Step 3 (option B): or report the event through the vendor ingest API
### directly (no FHIR store needed):
POST http://localhost:8081/subscription/events
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "resourceType": "Task",
  "resourceId": "demo-task-1",
  "interaction": "create",
  "ownerUra": "00000020",
  "useCaseCode": "308292007"
}

### Step 4: the notification arrives at Zonnebloem, normalised into the inbox.
### The focus is an OPAQUE alias (HMAC of the internal id) — HAPI's own ids
### fail the spec's id-only requirements (research question 8).
GET http://localhost:8181/subscription/inbox

### Step 5: follow-up pull — read the Task via its alias at Plataan's public
### FHIR surface (the knooppunt de-aliases and reads the tenant store; it is
### now in the data path — that is the finding, not an accident).
### Replace {{alias}} with the id from the inbox focus ("Task/<alias>").
GET http://localhost:8080/fhir/Task/{{alias}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Step 5b: acknowledge the inbox entry (at-least-once internal delivery).
### Replace 1 with the inbox entry id.
POST http://localhost:8181/subscription/inbox/1/ack

### Step 6: what actually travelled — the transport logs on both sides
### (the spec's logging obligation, queryable):
GET http://localhost:8081/subscription/log

###
GET http://localhost:8181/subscription/log

### Extra: gap recovery demo — lose an event on purpose (delivery suppressed),
### then send another; Zonnebloem detects the gap and recovers via $events.
POST http://localhost:8081/subscription/events
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "resourceType": "Task",
  "resourceId": "demo-task-1",
  "interaction": "update",
  "ownerUra": "00000020",
  "suppressDelivery": true
}

###
POST http://localhost:8081/subscription/events
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "resourceType": "Task",
  "resourceId": "demo-task-1",
  "interaction": "update",
  "ownerUra": "00000020"
}

### The inbox now shows all events, in order, gap filled ("recovered" in the log):
GET http://localhost:8181/subscription/inbox?all=true

### Extra: the wire-level server surface, for the curious (this is the part a
### vendor never has to touch):
GET http://localhost:8080/fhir/Subscription/{{subscriptionId}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

###
GET http://localhost:8080/fhir/Subscription/{{subscriptionId}}/$status
Accept: application/fhir+json

###
GET http://localhost:8080/fhir/Subscription/{{subscriptionId}}/$events?eventsSinceNumber=1&eventsUntilNumber=3
Accept: application/fhir+json
